                                               "id":  "var-let-const",
                                               "title":  "What is the difference between var, let and const?",
                                               "what":  "",
                                               "how":  "",
                                               "why":  "",
                                               "when":  "",
                                               "underTheHood":  "All three declarations create bindings during environment initialization. var bindings are initialized to undefined, while let and const remain uninitialized until their declarations execute, creating the Temporal Dead Zone.",
                                               "interviewAnswer":  "var is function-scoped, while let and const are block-scoped. var can be redeclared, whereas let and const cannot be redeclared in the same scope. const requires initialization and prevents reassignment of its binding.",
                                               "keywords":  [

                                                            ],
                                               "pitfalls":  [
                                                                "const does not make objects immutable.",
                                                                "var is function-scoped, not block-scoped.",
                                                                "Accessing let or const before initialization throws ReferenceError."
